Package: hypergraphs
Title: Higher-Order Network Analysis
Version: 0.7.9
Authors@R: c(
    person("Mohammed", "Saqr", email = "saqr@saqr.me", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-5881-3109"))
    )
Description: Build and analyze higher-order networks, in which a relation
    can involve more than two nodes or can depend on more than the current
    node. Covers the three structure families of the higher-order network
    literature reviewed by Battiston et al. (2020)
    <doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2020.05.004>, together with text hypergraphs,
    which represent a corpus as a hypergraph. The tables of every result are
    read with one accessor, and every function that takes sequences accepts
    long, wide, list or model input. Memory networks from categorical
    sequences: higher-order networks with rule extraction Xu,
    Wickramarathne, and Chawla (2016) <doi:10.1126/sciadv.1600028>,
    higher-order network embedding Saebi et al. (2020)
    <doi:10.1089/big.2019.0169>, hypergeometric path anomaly detection
    LaRock et al. (2020) <doi:10.1137/1.9781611976236.52>, the multi-order
    generative model Scholtes (2017) <doi:10.1145/3097983.3098145>,
    higher-order centralities Scholtes, Wider, and Garas (2016)
    <doi:10.1140/epjb/e2016-60663-0>, a permutation test of the Markov
    order, a path-dependence diagnostic, and bootstrap and permutation
    inference for extracted rules. Simplicial complexes: clique,
    Vietoris-Rips and pathway complexes, Betti numbers, persistent homology,
    persistence landscapes, bottleneck and Wasserstein distances, and
    q-analysis. Hypergraphs: construction from group memberships, sliding
    windows over sequences, network cliques, interval records or embedding
    nearest neighbours; structural measures; tensor eigenvector
    centralities Benson (2019) <doi:10.1137/18M1203031>; PageRank with
    edge-dependent vertex weights Chitra and Raphael (2019)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.1905.08287>; the weighted normalized hypergraph
    Laplacian of Zhou, Huang, and Scholkopf (2006)
    <doi:10.7551/mitpress/7503.003.0205> with spectral and symmetric
    non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) clustering Hayashi et al. (2020)
    <doi:10.1145/3340531.3412034> and transductive label spreading;
    projections to weighted graphs and s-line graphs; random hypergraphs;
    temporal snapshots; hyperedge betweenness and closeness; community
    detection with Infomap ensembles and the quality of partitions; motifs
    of three hyperedges; and null models by degree-preserving swaps and the
    configuration model. Text hypergraphs: a corpus becomes a weighted
    document-word hypergraph in either orientation (bag of words with
    smoothed term frequency-inverse document frequency (tf-idf) weights,
    token windows, sentences, or embedding nearest neighbors), analyzed by
    clustering, keyword extraction, topic models and few-label
    classification.
